WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.438
Several papers satisfy the same Boolean

00:00:03.458 --> 00:00:05.977
conditions. They are all eligible.

00:00:06.126 --> 00:00:08.361
Does that make them equally good matches?

00:00:08.536 --> 00:00:11.222
And if not, what evidence should put one

00:00:11.242 --> 00:00:12.574
paper above another?

00:00:12.724 --> 00:00:13.734
Make a prediction.

00:00:16.152 --> 00:00:18.980
Ranked retrieval assigns graded scores and

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.435
orders the candidates.

00:00:20.623 --> 00:00:22.575
That describes an operation, not a

00:00:22.595 --> 00:00:24.843
formula. Scores might use lexical

00:00:24.863 --> 00:00:28.116
evidence, vectors or several signals.

00:00:28.278 --> 00:00:31.247
Chapter Three examines one lexical method:

00:00:31.397 --> 00:00:33.103
BM twenty-five.

00:00:34.013 --> 00:00:37.155
BM twenty-five adds weighted evidence from

00:00:37.175 --> 00:00:39.655
analysed terms shared by the query and

00:00:39.675 --> 00:00:43.237
document. For delulu plus job, the text

00:00:43.257 --> 00:00:45.505
unrealistic job expectations has a job

00:00:45.525 --> 00:00:48.663
match. It has no delulu match, so that

00:00:48.683 --> 00:00:50.505
term contributes nothing.

00:00:51.088 --> 00:00:53.029
Matching another query term can add

00:00:53.049 --> 00:00:54.394
another contribution.

00:00:54.582 --> 00:00:56.997
But matches are not equally informative.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:58.815
The next question is how much a term

00:00:58.835 --> 00:01:01.186
discriminates among records in the

00:01:01.206 --> 00:01:04.356
collection. No query expansion is assumed

00:01:04.376 --> 00:01:05.489
in this example.

00:01:06.397 --> 00:01:08.809
Use the chapter's illustrative collection

00:01:08.829 --> 00:01:10.200
of ten records.

00:01:10.362 --> 00:01:12.043
Delulu occurs in two.

00:01:12.231 --> 00:01:13.538
Job occurs in nine.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:16.386
All else equal, which matching term

00:01:16.432 --> 00:01:18.357
narrows the possibilities more?

00:01:20.061 --> 00:01:22.506
The rarer term usually receives more

00:01:22.526 --> 00:01:25.237
weight through inverse document frequency,

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:28.421
or IDF. Informative here means

00:01:28.441 --> 00:01:30.894
discriminating among documents.

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:32.738
It does not guarantee that a paper is

00:01:32.758 --> 00:01:35.366
useful for the reader's actual task.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.609
Document frequency counts how many

00:01:38.629 --> 00:01:40.258
documents contain the term.

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:43.003
Repeating a word twenty times in one paper

00:01:43.023 --> 00:01:45.516
still adds just one document to that

00:01:45.536 --> 00:01:48.944
count. Term frequency counts occurrences

00:01:48.964 --> 00:01:51.740
inside the individual document.

00:01:52.288 --> 00:01:53.683
Those are different signals.

00:01:53.845 --> 00:01:56.196
Collection rarity supplies a weight.

00:01:56.358 --> 00:01:58.800
Within-document repetition supplies

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:01.840
evidence whose contribution will saturate.

00:02:02.003 --> 00:02:04.483
Confusing the two counts makes the score

00:02:04.503 --> 00:02:06.054
harder to interpret.

00:02:06.971 --> 00:02:09.825
For a labeled positive-IDF variant, the

00:02:09.845 --> 00:02:12.454
two-of-ten term gets a weight of about one

00:02:12.474 --> 00:02:13.549
point four eight.

00:02:13.724 --> 00:02:16.127
The nine-of-ten term gets about zero point

00:02:16.147 --> 00:02:19.129
one five. Production variants can differ;

00:02:19.317 --> 00:02:21.526
these are calculated teaching values.

00:02:22.062 --> 00:02:24.129
At average document length, with one

00:02:24.149 --> 00:02:26.332
occurrence of each term, the frequency

00:02:26.352 --> 00:02:29.798
factor is one. Add the two contributions:

00:02:29.844 --> 00:02:31.783
about one point six three.

00:02:31.958 --> 00:02:34.283
Different repetition and length change

00:02:34.303 --> 00:02:35.700
those contributions.

00:02:35.875 --> 00:02:38.562
The total is a score, not a probability of

00:02:38.582 --> 00:02:39.476
relevance.

00:02:40.365 --> 00:02:42.674
Now consider the text unrealistic

00:02:42.694 --> 00:02:45.716
expectations. It shares neither analysed

00:02:45.736 --> 00:02:48.408
query term, delulu nor job.

00:02:48.558 --> 00:02:51.926
In our plain, unexpanded lexical example,

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.877
neither term contributes evidence.

00:02:55.053 --> 00:02:58.305
Query expansion could change what matches;

00:02:58.467 --> 00:03:01.051
the scoring formula alone does not bridge

00:03:01.071 --> 00:03:02.635
every vocabulary gap.

00:03:03.195 --> 00:03:05.414
A high score is also not a verdict about

00:03:05.434 --> 00:03:08.146
usefulness. A well-matching paper can

00:03:08.166 --> 00:03:11.097
concern the wrong population or fail to

00:03:11.117 --> 00:03:12.295
answer the task.

00:03:12.457 --> 00:03:15.581
Ask about match, rarity, repetition and

00:03:15.601 --> 00:03:18.133
length. The next film tests why twenty

00:03:18.153 --> 00:03:20.723
repetitions do not earn twenty times the

00:03:20.743 --> 00:03:21.444
credit.
